  "preamble": "Proto-Germanic *þ [θ] became [d] across continental West Germanic. This is not part of the High German consonant shift proper but a later change that spread from the south between the 8th and 11th centuries into Old High German, then reached Dutch and Low German. In Continental Scandinavian, *þ became t in most words but d in unstressed function words such as du 'you'.",
